= Chilli =

Heat the @olive oil{2%tbsp} in a #large heavy-based saucepan with a lid{} and fry the @onion{2}(chopped) and @garlic{2%cloves}(crushed) until softened. Increase the heat and add the @lean beef mince{1%kg}, cooking quickly until browned and breaking down any chunks of meat with a wooden spoon.

Pour in the @red wine{250%ml} and boil for 2 to ~{3%minutes}.

Stir in the @chopped tomatoes{2%tins × 400 g}, @tomato purée{3%tbsp}, @red chillies{2}(thinly sliced) (or @?dried chilli flakes{4%tsp}), @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @ground coriander{1%tsp}, @cinnamon{1%stick}, and @Worcestershire sauce{good shake}, and crumble in the @beef stock cube{1}. Season well with @salt and @black pepper{}(freshly ground). Bring to a simmer, cover with a lid and cook over a gentle heat for about 50 minutes to ~{1%hour}, stirring occasionally until the mixture is rich and thickened.

> Increase quantity of chillies when preparing for myself.

Add the @kidney beans{2%tins × 400 g}, @fresh coriander{large bunch}(roughly chopped) and @?plain chocolate{100%g}. Cook for a further ~{10%minutes}, uncovered, before removing from the heat, adding any extra seasoning if necessary.

= Serve =

Serve with @?lime{}(wedges), @rice, @./Savoury/Guacamole{}, @sour cream{}, and a @big green salad{}.

> The chilli is much tastier a day or two after it's cooked because the flavours develop and the texture becomes richer. Simply leave to cool, stick in the fridge and gently heat before serving. If you are eating the chilli on the day you prepared it, any leftovers can be frozen in individual portions in well-sealed sandwich bags, then reheated by boiling from frozen in a saucepan of water for about 15-20 minutes until steaming hot.